1
00:00:01,550 --> 00:00:06,161
The machine at the heart of Claude Code, the
agent that's quietly eating the

2
00:00:06,194 --> 00:00:09,146
software industry, is about one
hundred lines of code.

3
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:10,141
Not the model.

4
00:00:10,175 --> 00:00:11,800
The model is a giant.

5
00:00:11,833 --> 00:00:14,122
I mean the machine wrapped around it.

6
00:00:14,155 --> 00:00:14,786
The loop.

7
00:00:14,819 --> 00:00:19,761
And today, we build that machine from an empty
file, into a real agent that

8
00:00:19,794 --> 00:00:23,410
hunts down a bug and fixes it, live,
while we watch.

9
00:00:23,443 --> 00:00:28,386
Stay to the end, because I'll tell you a secret
about this exact video that

10
00:00:28,419 --> 00:00:29,713
proves the machine works.

11
00:00:41,146 --> 00:00:42,793
Here's the build plan.

12
00:00:42,836 --> 00:00:44,906
On the table, a skeleton.

13
00:00:44,948 --> 00:00:47,018
An agent with no parts.

14
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:49,552
On the wall, everything it needs.

15
00:00:49,594 --> 00:00:51,664
A heartbeat, called the loop.

16
00:00:51,706 --> 00:00:52,931
Hands, called tools.

17
00:00:52,974 --> 00:00:54,621
A gate, called permissions.

18
00:00:54,663 --> 00:00:56,311
A memory, called context.

19
00:00:56,353 --> 00:00:58,845
We install them one by one.

20
00:00:58,888 --> 00:01:03,914
Then we bolt on the upgrades from this
series, and switch it

21
00:01:03,957 --> 00:01:04,337
on.

22
00:01:09,579 --> 00:01:11,130
Start with the heartbeat.

23
00:01:11,170 --> 00:01:13,517
You send the model two things.

24
00:01:13,557 --> 00:01:18,290
Your request, and a list of tools it's
allowed to ask for.

25
00:01:18,330 --> 00:01:21,473
The model replies with one of two things.

26
00:01:21,512 --> 00:01:23,461
An answer, or a request.

27
00:01:23,501 --> 00:01:26,246
Please run this tool, with these inputs.

28
00:01:26,286 --> 00:01:31,417
Your code runs the tool, appends the result to
the conversation, and sends

29
00:01:31,457 --> 00:01:33,008
the whole thing back.

30
00:01:33,048 --> 00:01:34,201
Answer, or request.

31
00:01:34,241 --> 00:01:34,599
Again.

32
00:01:34,639 --> 00:01:35,395
And again.

33
00:01:35,434 --> 00:01:37,384
Until the model stops asking.

34
00:01:37,423 --> 00:01:39,770
That loop is the entire secret.

35
00:01:39,810 --> 00:01:44,941
Every AI agent on earth, including the ones
that feel like magic, is

36
00:01:44,981 --> 00:01:46,930
this loop wearing different clothes.

37
00:01:50,170 --> 00:01:53,659
And in real code, the heartbeat is
embarrassingly small.

38
00:01:53,698 --> 00:01:54,835
One while loop.

39
00:01:54,874 --> 00:01:56,011
Call the model.

40
00:01:56,050 --> 00:02:01,107
If it asked for a tool, run it, append
the result, and go

41
00:02:01,147 --> 00:02:01,891
around again.

42
00:02:01,931 --> 00:02:03,851
If it didn't, you're done.

43
00:02:03,891 --> 00:02:08,556
Anthropic's own tutorial builds this complete
working loop in about eighty lines.

44
00:02:08,595 --> 00:02:11,692
You could read the whole thing
over coffee.

45
00:02:16,931 --> 00:02:18,092
Now the hands.

46
00:02:18,132 --> 00:02:20,895
A tool is three pieces of text.

47
00:02:20,935 --> 00:02:21,695
A name.

48
00:02:21,735 --> 00:02:26,900
A description, which is how the model
decides when to reach for it.

49
00:02:26,940 --> 00:02:30,503
And a schema, describing exactly what
inputs it takes.

50
00:02:30,543 --> 00:02:31,704
Notice what's missing.

51
00:02:31,745 --> 00:02:33,706
The model never executes anything.

52
00:02:33,746 --> 00:02:34,107
Ever.

53
00:02:34,147 --> 00:02:39,311
It writes a request, your harness does the
deed, and hands back the

54
00:02:39,351 --> 00:02:39,712
result.

55
00:02:39,752 --> 00:02:42,915
The AI is the brain in the jar.

56
00:02:42,955 --> 00:02:47,319
You built its hands, so you decide
what they can hold.

57
00:02:47,359 --> 00:02:52,524
Give it read file, list files, edit file, and run
command, and congratulations.

58
00:02:52,564 --> 00:02:54,125
That's a coding agent.

59
00:02:59,365 --> 00:03:02,855
But before you switch it on, the gate.

60
00:03:02,899 --> 00:03:06,831
Every tool request passes through a
checkpoint you control.

61
00:03:06,875 --> 00:03:08,156
Reading a file?

62
00:03:08,201 --> 00:03:09,482
Fine, allowed automatically.

63
00:03:09,526 --> 00:03:09,924
Editing?

64
00:03:09,968 --> 00:03:13,016
Maybe auto approve inside the
project folder.

65
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:16,109
Deleting files, touching the network,
installing software?

66
00:03:16,153 --> 00:03:17,876
Ask the human first.

67
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:19,201
The model requests.

68
00:03:19,245 --> 00:03:20,526
The harness decides.

69
00:03:20,571 --> 00:03:25,828
That one sentence is the entire security model
of every serious agent,

70
00:03:25,872 --> 00:03:27,595
and now it's yours.

71
00:03:32,839 --> 00:03:33,959
One more organ.

72
00:03:33,997 --> 00:03:34,345
Memory.

73
00:03:34,383 --> 00:03:38,977
Every tool result gets appended to the
conversation, which means the context

74
00:03:39,016 --> 00:03:44,382
window, that ring from episode one, is filling the
whole time the agent works.

75
00:03:44,420 --> 00:03:49,786
A real harness watches the ring, and when it
gets close to full, it

76
00:03:49,825 --> 00:03:50,172
compacts.

77
00:03:50,211 --> 00:03:53,647
Summarizes the old, keeps the essentials,
and keeps working.

78
00:03:53,685 --> 00:03:58,279
Install that, and your agent can grind for
hours instead of minutes.

79
00:04:01,518 --> 00:04:03,002
Look at the table.

80
00:04:03,040 --> 00:04:03,764
A loop.

81
00:04:03,802 --> 00:04:04,144
Tools.

82
00:04:04,182 --> 00:04:04,905
A gate.

83
00:04:04,943 --> 00:04:05,667
A memory.

84
00:04:05,705 --> 00:04:10,995
Under two hundred lines, most of it plain
text, and you have a genuine

85
00:04:11,033 --> 00:04:11,756
coding agent.

86
00:04:11,794 --> 00:04:14,420
This is the moment most tutorials end.

87
00:04:14,459 --> 00:04:17,465
But you didn't come here for a toy.

88
00:04:17,503 --> 00:04:19,749
So here's the professional's cheat code.

89
00:04:25,187 --> 00:04:30,240
It's called the Claude Agent SDK, and it is
literally Claude Code as a

90
00:04:30,276 --> 00:04:30,603
library.

91
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:35,329
The same loop, the same tools, the same permission
gate and context management

92
00:04:35,366 --> 00:04:38,964
that power the real product, programmable
from Python or TypeScript.

93
00:04:39,001 --> 00:04:43,690
Everything we just built by hand, production grade,
battle tested, in one import.

94
00:04:43,727 --> 00:04:45,871
You bring the part that matters.

95
00:04:45,908 --> 00:04:49,507
Who your agent is, and what it's
allowed to do.

96
00:04:54,743 --> 00:04:57,422
So let's build ours, for real.

97
00:04:57,467 --> 00:04:58,330
Meet wired.

98
00:04:58,376 --> 00:04:59,238
Our agent.

99
00:04:59,284 --> 00:05:01,963
Here is every line we wrote.

100
00:05:02,008 --> 00:05:04,233
A system prompt, ten lines.

101
00:05:04,279 --> 00:05:07,866
You are wired, a small, careful
coding agent.

102
00:05:07,911 --> 00:05:09,682
Read before you edit.

103
00:05:09,727 --> 00:05:11,498
Verify after you change.

104
00:05:11,544 --> 00:05:14,677
Four allowed tools, and not one more.

105
00:05:14,722 --> 00:05:16,493
Read, edit, run, search.

106
00:05:16,539 --> 00:05:19,218
Auto approve edits, gate everything else.

107
00:05:19,263 --> 00:05:24,666
And its entire world is one demo folder,
running on Claude Sonnet

108
00:05:24,712 --> 00:05:25,120
five.

109
00:05:25,166 --> 00:05:30,569
About fifty lines of our code, on top
of the machine you

110
00:05:30,615 --> 00:05:31,932
now understand completely.

111
00:05:35,177 --> 00:05:37,052
Time to switch it on.

112
00:05:37,090 --> 00:05:42,410
The task: run temps dot p y, find the bug,
fix it, and verify.

113
00:05:42,448 --> 00:05:43,940
Watch the loop breathe.

114
00:05:43,979 --> 00:05:46,619
First heartbeat, it searches for the file.

115
00:05:46,658 --> 00:05:48,150
Second, it reads it.

116
00:05:48,188 --> 00:05:51,977
Third, it runs it, and sees the
output is wrong.

117
00:05:52,015 --> 00:05:56,952
Thirty seven Celsius should be ninety eight point
six Fahrenheit, and it's not.

118
00:05:56,990 --> 00:05:58,865
Then it spots the crime.

119
00:05:58,903 --> 00:06:01,161
The conversion formula is upside down.

120
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:04,223
Five ninths, where it should be
nine fifths.

121
00:06:07,061 --> 00:06:09,924
So it asks for the edit tool.

122
00:06:09,966 --> 00:06:11,169
The gate approves.

123
00:06:11,211 --> 00:06:12,414
One line changes.

124
00:06:12,456 --> 00:06:16,149
It runs the file again, and there it is.

125
00:06:16,191 --> 00:06:16,979
Thirty two.

126
00:06:17,021 --> 00:06:17,809
Two twelve.

127
00:06:17,851 --> 00:06:19,469
Ninety eight point six.

128
00:06:19,511 --> 00:06:24,864
Then it reports back in one sentence, exactly as
its system prompt demands.

129
00:06:24,905 --> 00:06:27,769
And I want to be completely clear.

130
00:06:27,810 --> 00:06:29,429
Nothing here was staged.

131
00:06:29,470 --> 00:06:34,824
That was the actual transcript of our fifty
line agent, doing real work.

132
